From: DashBerry <zero/README.md>
Subject: [PATCH] uvc: tell the gadget the frame interval, and overspeed it

f_uvc sizes its USB requests from video->interval, and since Linux 6.13 that
one number decides how many isochronous slots a video frame is cut across:

    uvc_video_prep_requests():
        nreq = DIV_ROUND_UP(video->interval, 2^(bInterval-1) * 1250)
    uvc_buffer_prepare():
        req_payload_size = (bytesused + nreq * 12) / nreq

video->interval has EXACTLY ONE WRITER in the whole driver - uvc_v4l2_s_parm(),
i.e. VIDIOC_S_PARM on the UVC node - and that interface only arrived upstream in
October 2024 (2fe7c94dcd09). This daemon predates it and never calls it, so
video->interval keeps the value uvcg_video_init() left there:

    video->interval = 666666;       /* 15 fps */

On a high-speed gadget at bInterval 1 that is 534 requests per frame. A 42 KB
H.264 frame becomes 534 packets of 91 bytes, one per 125 us microframe, taking
66 ms of wire time for a frame the sensor delivers every 33 ms. Three
consequences, all of which were measured on a Pi Zero W before the cause was
known:

  - The gadget cannot deliver more than 15 fps, whatever else is tuned. The
    frame is spread over video->interval by construction, so bInterval,
    maxpacket and buffer count all leave the ceiling exactly where it is.

  - All 534 slots must land. On dwc2 a missed slot completes -ENODATA, f_uvc
    sets UVC_QUEUE_DROP_INCOMPLETE, and uvc_video_encode_isoc() then ends the
    buffer where it stands - so one miss discards the rest of the frame. 711 of
    745 frames arrived truncated, and every recording decoded as a wall of
    "bytestream -5" and "concealing N DC, N AC, N MV errors".

  - req_payload_size is unbounded before Linux 6.19 (upstream 2edc1acb1a25), so
    a frame larger than nreq * (req_size - 12) overruns the request buffer.

The interval is already in hand: uvc_fill_streaming_control() reads it from the
frame's dwDefaultFrameInterval and puts it in the streaming control it answers
the host with. This hands the same value to the gadget, at COMMIT/SET time,
which is after the format is applied and before STREAMON - and STREAMON is when
uvc_video_alloc_requests() reads it.

AND THEN DIVIDES IT, because the true interval is not a safe transmission plan.
USB slots arrive every 125 us regardless of what we report, so nreq is also the
number of microframes a frame occupies: 267 slots for a 33.33 ms frame period
is 33.375 ms of wire, a 100.1% duty cycle with no slack at all. Measured
2026-08-15 with the interval correct and the divisor at 1, on 720p30 H.264:

    frames in the recording                689
    frames arriving as 39-63 byte stubs     96   in bursts of six, one
                                                 timestamp per burst - the
                                                 queued buffers being flushed
    packets carrying UVC_STREAM_ERR        213   ~1.9 per stub frame, i.e.
                                                 f_uvc truncated them itself;
                                                 the host lost nothing

Halving the reported interval halves nreq: the frame drains in 16.8 ms and
leaves 16.5 ms of idle wire behind it, while still offering 135 KB per frame
against a measured worst frame of 24 KB. The divisor is read from
/boot/uvc-overspeed, like /boot/uvc-nbufs and /boot/uvc-interval, so it can be
swept on the card.

Divisor 2 was then measured, and it does cut truncation - stub frames 96 -> 22,
worst burst 6 -> 3, decoder errors 125 -> 70, median slice 11652 -> 18241 bytes -
while collapsing delivery to 6.02 fps, with the capture freezing for about a
minute mid-run and taking 114 s to collect 690 frames that take 23 s at divisor
1. Nothing in uvc_video_prep_requests(), uvcg_video_pump() or
uvcg_video_hw_submit() accounts for a stall of that length, and nothing else in
the driver reads video->interval, so THE MECHANISM IS NOT UNDERSTOOD.

THE DEFAULT IS THEREFORE 1, which is the configuration measured at 30.6 fps. 2
stays reachable because it is the only change so far that has moved truncation.
Do not raise the default until the stall has an explanation.

video->interval reaches no descriptor - only uvc_video_prep_requests() and
VIDIOC_G_PARM read it - so the host cannot see the divisor and the negotiated
dwFrameInterval is unchanged.

Applies to peterbay/uvc-gadget @ e9a733fe5c4a7fcb48e963e8d994bc33d24d814e,
the SHA package/piwebcam/piwebcam.mk pins.
---
diff --git a/uvc-gadget.c b/uvc-gadget.c
--- a/uvc-gadget.c
+++ b/uvc-gadget.c
@@ -894,6 +894,146 @@
     return v4l2_get_format(dev);
 }
 
+/*
+ * How much faster than real time to tell the gadget it is streaming.
+ *
+ * nreq is derived from video->interval, and USB slots arrive every 125 us no
+ * matter what, so nreq is also the number of microframes a frame occupies. At
+ * the true interval that is DIV_ROUND_UP(333333, 1250) = 267 slots for a
+ * 33.33 ms frame period - 33.375 ms of wire, a 100.1% duty cycle with no slack
+ * whatsoever. A missed isochronous slot then has nowhere to be made up: f_uvc
+ * sets UVC_QUEUE_DROP_INCOMPLETE and uvc_video_encode_isoc() ends the buffer
+ * where it stands.
+ *
+ * Reporting a shorter interval than reality shrinks nreq proportionally, so the
+ * frame drains early and leaves idle wire behind it for the pump to recover in.
+ * Byte capacity - nreq * (req_size - 12) - falls with it, but there is a lot to
+ * give away:
+ *
+ *      divisor   nreq   drain     slack      capacity   vs 8 Mbps @ 30 fps
+ *          1      267   33.4 ms    -0.1 ms    270 KB       8.1x
+ *          2      134   16.8 ms   +16.5 ms    135 KB       4.1x
+ *          3       89   11.1 ms   +22.2 ms     90 KB       2.7x
+ *
+ * Measured 2026-08-15 at divisor 1 (i.e. before this knob existed): 96 of 689
+ * frames arrived as 39-63 byte stubs, in bursts of six, each burst sharing one
+ * timestamp - the six queued buffers being flushed after a stall. 213 packets
+ * carried UVC_STREAM_ERR, ~1.9 per stub frame, which is f_uvc reporting that it
+ * truncated them itself rather than the host losing anything.
+ *
+ * Divisor 2 was then measured on the same bench. It cuts truncation exactly as
+ * the arithmetic says it should - and breaks pacing in a way the arithmetic does
+ * not explain at all:
+ *
+ *                          divisor 1        divisor 2
+ *      stub frames         96 (13.9%)       22 (3.2%)
+ *      worst stub burst    6                3
+ *      median slice        11652 B          18241 B
+ *      decoder errors      125              70
+ *      delivered rate      30.6 fps         6.0 fps
+ *
+ * At divisor 2 the capture ran about 80 frames, froze for roughly a minute,
+ * then resumed, and took 114 s to collect the 690 frames that take 23 s at
+ * divisor 1. Nothing in uvc_video_prep_requests(), uvcg_video_pump() or
+ * uvcg_video_hw_submit() accounts for a stall of that length, and nothing else
+ * in the driver reads video->interval, so THE MECHANISM IS NOT UNDERSTOOD.
+ *
+ * THE DEFAULT IS THEREFORE 1 - the configuration known to deliver 30 fps - and
+ * 2 is left reachable because it is the only thing so far that has moved
+ * truncation. Do not raise the default until the stall has an explanation.
+ * It is read from /boot so sweeping it costs a card edit rather than an image
+ * rebuild, the same as /boot/uvc-nbufs and /boot/uvc-interval.
+ */
+#define UVC_OVERSPEED_PATH    "/boot/uvc-overspeed"
+#define UVC_OVERSPEED_DEFAULT 1
+#define UVC_OVERSPEED_MAX     8
+
+static unsigned int uvc_overspeed(void)
+{
+    static unsigned int cached = 0;
+    unsigned int value;
+    FILE * fp;
+
+    if (cached) {
+        return cached;
+    }
+
+    cached = UVC_OVERSPEED_DEFAULT;
+
+    fp = fopen(UVC_OVERSPEED_PATH, "r");
+    if (fp) {
+        if (fscanf(fp, "%u", &value) == 1
+            && value >= 1 && value <= UVC_OVERSPEED_MAX) {
+            cached = value;
+        }
+        fclose(fp);
+    }
+
+    return cached;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Tell the UVC gadget the frame interval it is streaming at.
+ *
+ * f_uvc sizes its USB requests from video->interval, and since Linux 6.13 that
+ * decides how many isochronous slots a frame is cut across:
+ *
+ *     nreq = DIV_ROUND_UP(video->interval, 2^(bInterval-1) * 1250)
+ *     req_payload_size = (bytesused + nreq * 12) / nreq
+ *
+ * video->interval has exactly one writer in the whole driver - VIDIOC_S_PARM on
+ * the UVC node - and nothing here ever called it, so it kept the value
+ * uvcg_video_init() left there: 666666, i.e. 15 fps. A 30 fps frame was then
+ * spread over 534 requests of 91 bytes and 66 ms of wire time, the gadget could
+ * not deliver more than 15 fps whatever else was tuned, and a single missed
+ * isochronous slot out of the 534 truncated the frame - which is what the host
+ * saw as every frame decoding short.
+ *
+ * The interval is already in hand: uvc_fill_streaming_control() reads it from
+ * the frame's dwDefaultFrameInterval. This just hands it over.
+ */
+static int v4l2_apply_frame_interval(struct v4l2_device * dev, unsigned int frame_interval)
+{
+    struct v4l2_streamparm parm;
+    unsigned int overspeed;
+    unsigned int planned;
+    int ret;
+
+    if (dev->is_streaming || !dev->fd || !frame_interval) {
+        return -EINVAL;
+    }
+
+    /*
+     * What we report is a transmission plan, not a claim about the sensor.
+     * video->interval is read by uvc_video_prep_requests() and returned by
+     * VIDIOC_G_PARM, and by nothing else - it reaches no descriptor, so the
+     * host cannot see it and the negotiated dwFrameInterval is unaffected.
+     */
+    overspeed = uvc_overspeed();
+    planned = frame_interval / overspeed;
+    if (!planned) {
+        planned = frame_interval;
+    }
+
+    CLEAR(parm);
+    parm.type = dev->buffer_type;
+    parm.parm.output.timeperframe.numerator   = planned;
+    parm.parm.output.timeperframe.denominator = 10000000;
+
+    ret = ioctl(dev->fd, VIDIOC_S_PARM, &parm);
+    if (ret < 0) {
+        printf("%s: Unable to set frame interval %u: %s (%d).\n",
+            dev->device_type_name, planned, strerror(errno), errno);
+        return ret;
+    }
+
+    printf("%s: Setting frame interval to: %u (%u fps), real %u (%u fps), overspeed %ux\n",
+        dev->device_type_name, planned, 10000000 / planned,
+        frame_interval, 10000000 / frame_interval, overspeed);
+
+    return 0;
+}
+
 static void v4l2_set_ctrl_value(struct control_mapping_pair ctrl, unsigned int ctrl_v4l2, int v4l2_ctrl_value)
 {
     struct v4l2_queryctrl queryctrl;
@@ -1602,6 +1742,12 @@
             v4l2_apply_format(&v4l2_dev, frame_format->video_format, frame_format->wWidth, frame_format->wHeight);
         }
         v4l2_apply_format(&uvc_dev, frame_format->video_format, frame_format->wWidth, frame_format->wHeight);
+        /*
+         * ...and the frame interval, which is NOT part of the format. Without
+         * it f_uvc keeps its 15 fps default and cuts every frame into 534
+         * requests. See v4l2_apply_frame_interval().
+         */
+        v4l2_apply_frame_interval(&uvc_dev, frame_interval);
     }
 }
 
